---
import fs from "node:fs";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Prose from "../../components/Prose.astro";
import GuideData from "../../components/GuideData.astro";
import EveryCut from "../../components/EveryCut.astro";
import { bySlug, kicker } from "../../dossiers/registry.js";
import { dateStamp } from "../../dossiers/display.js";
const D = bySlug["farms"];
const load = (slug) => JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(`data/series/${slug}.json`, "utf8"));
// The state tables, the long state series and the climate guide's monthly rainfall are large;
// the page carries build-time picks of what the plates draw. The whole files are in the
// downloads and the every-series table below (CONTRACT-render: page JS budget).
const lastIdx = (a) => { for (let i = a.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) if (a[i] != null) return i; return -1; };
const stateName = (label) => label.split(":")[0].trim();
// the states guide's Handbook of Statistics on Indian States tables (read only): output by crop
// and yield, the latest year every table shares
const HBS = { rice: "t058", wheat: "t059", coarse_cereals: "t060", pulses: "t061", foodgrains: "t062", oilseeds: "t063", cotton: "t064", sugarcane: "t065", jute_mesta: "t066" };
const hbsLatest = (t) => {
  const b = load(`states-hbs-${t}`), i = b.dates.length - 1;
  return { year: b.dates[i], unit: b.meta.unit, ai: b.series.all_india?.data[i] ?? null,
    rows: Object.entries(b.series).filter(([k, s]) => k !== "all_india" && s.data[i] != null).map(([k, s]) => ({ k, name: stateName(s.label), v: s.data[i] })) };
};
const SP = load("farms-states-production");
const LONG_KEYS = ["all_india__wheat", "punjab__wheat", "haryana__wheat", "uttar_pradesh__wheat", "uttar_pradesh_undivided__wheat", "madhya_pradesh__wheat", "madhya_pradesh_undivided__wheat", "rajasthan__wheat",
  "all_india__rice", "punjab__rice", "west_bengal__rice", "uttar_pradesh__rice", "uttar_pradesh_undivided__rice", "andhra_pradesh__rice", "andhra_pradesh_undivided__rice", "telangana__rice", "odisha__rice", "chhattisgarh__rice", "madhya_pradesh_undivided__rice"];
const FERT = load("farms-states-fertiliser");
const fi = FERT.dates.length - 1;
const CRU = load("climate-cru-monthly");
const jjas = {};
CRU.dates.forEach((d, i) => { const m = +d.slice(5, 7), y = d.slice(0, 4); if (m >= 6 && m <= 9) { (jjas[y] ??= []).push(CRU.series.pr.data[i]); } });
const CPIAL = load("cpialrl-1986");
const PICK = {
  states: Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(HBS).map(([k, t]) => [k, hbsLatest(t)])),
  yields: { rice: hbsLatest("t080"), wheat: hbsLatest("t081") },
  long: { dates: SP.dates, series: Object.fromEntries(LONG_KEYS.filter((k) => SP.series[k]).map((k) => [k, { label: SP.series[k].label, data: SP.series[k].data }])) },
  fert: { year: FERT.dates[fi], dates: FERT.dates, rows: Object.entries(FERT.series).map(([k, s]) => ({ k, name: (s.state ?? stateName(s.label)).replace(/ \(average\)$/, ""), v: s.data[fi], first: s.data[0] })),
    ai: Object.entries(FERT.series).find(([k]) => /all_india/.test(k))?.[1].data ?? [] },
  monsoon: Object.entries(jjas).filter(([, v]) => v.length === 4 && v.every((x) => x != null)).map(([y, v]) => ({ y: +y, mm: Math.round(v.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) * 10) / 10 })),
  cpial: { dates: CPIAL.dates, al: CPIAL.series.al.data },
};
const pickJson = JSON.stringify(PICK).replace(/</g, "\\u003c");
---
<Base
  title="Farms and food · India Atlas"
  description={D.description}
  nav="guides"
  dateStamp={dateStamp(D)}
  crumbLeft={`<a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / ${D.section} / <b>${D.crumbTitle}</b>`}
  crumbRight={`${D.statusNote}, <b>${D.version}</b>`}
  canonical="https://atlas.thisindianlife.today/guides/farms/"
  ogImage={`/og/${D.slug}.png`}
>
<script type="application/json" id="farms-data" set:html={pickJson}></script>
<div class="page">

  <div class="titleblock break">
    <div class="kicker">{kicker(D)}</div>
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <h1 set:html={D.headline} />
      <p class="lede">The rice on the plate, the dal in the pot, the sugar in the tea and the cotton in the shirt start on about fourteen crore hectares of Indian fields. This guide counts what those fields grow each year, how much a hectare gives, which states grow what, the prices the government promises farmers, and the grain it buys, stores and hands out.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="meta">
      <span>Source <b>Agriculture and food ministries' statistics, as the RBI publishes them (DBIE)</b></span>
      <span>Frequency <b>Annual, crop years (July to June)</b></span>
      <span>Rulers <b>Final estimates and the latest advance estimate, never joined</b></span>
      <span>Latest <b id="m-latest">–</b></span>
    </div>
    <div class="numbers">
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Foodgrains harvested, final estimate</div><div class="v acc" id="n-fg">–</div><div class="d" id="d-fg">–</div><div class="nspark" id="nspark-fg"></div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Wheat from one hectare</div><div class="v" id="n-yield">–</div><div class="d" id="d-yield">–</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Support price for paddy, a quintal</div><div class="v" id="n-msp">–</div><div class="d" id="d-msp">–</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Grain in the central pool, 1 April</div><div class="v" id="n-stock">–</div><div class="d" id="d-stock">–</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- § 01 the current reading: generated prose (pipeline/slots.yml, farms). Inputs: farms-production (current), farms-yield, farms-area; facts cross.farms (farms-advance, farms-msp, farms-pds, farms-pds-latest) -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>The current reading</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit" id="u-reading">–</span></div>
    <div class="reading-top">
      <div><span class="rk">Foodgrains, final estimate</span><div class="big" id="rb-fg">–</div></div>
      <p class="rt-cap">Rice, wheat, coarse cereals and pulses harvested in the crop year <span id="rt-fy">–</span>, in million tonnes, on the agriculture ministry's final estimates.</p>
      <div class="rt-side"><span class="rk" id="rb-side-l">–</span><div class="rt-side-v" id="rb-side">–</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="reading-cols">
      <div class="reading-col">
        <span class="mk">What the figures say</span>
        <Prose slot="farms.current-reading.what-the-print-says" />
      </div>
      <div class="reading-col now">
        <span class="mk">What they do not say</span>
        <Prose slot="farms.current-reading.what-it-does-not-say" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 lived reality: from a hectare to a family's income. Inputs: farms-yield (wheat), farms-msp (wheat) -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>From one hectare to the plate</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Wheat, yield times the support price, rupees a hectare</span></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>A hectare is a square a hundred metres on each side, about two and a half acres. Most Indian farms are smaller than that. On it, a wheat farmer in an average year now harvests about <b id="lr-yield">–</b> of grain. If every kilo were sold to the government at the minimum support price, that hectare would bring in <b id="lr-gross">–</b> before a rupee is spent on seed, fertiliser, diesel, water, labour or rent.</p>
      <p>That is the gross figure, and it is generous. Only some farmers sell to the government at all: procurement is concentrated in a few states, and elsewhere wheat often sells below the support price. The costs come out of it. A family with half a hectare and one crop of wheat lives on a fraction of this. The chart shows how the gross figure has grown since 1975-76, in the rupees of each year.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-hectare" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>A hectare of wheat sold at the support price brings in far more rupees than it used to</h3>
            <div class="sub">All India wheat yield (kg per hectare) times the wheat minimum support price (rupees a kg), by crop year: what one average hectare would fetch at the support price, before costs, in the rupees of each year (not adjusted for inflation). Computed by India Atlas.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-hectare">–</div><div class="c" id="pc-hectare">–</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-hectare" style="position:relative"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Agriculture ministry, yield per hectare and minimum support prices, as published by the RBI (DBIE reports 886 and 461). Product computed by India Atlas.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>A quintal is 100 kg, so a support price of ₹2,000 a quintal is ₹20 a kg. The line multiplies the year's national average yield by that price. It is the most a farmer on an average hectare could have earned from wheat at the support price that year. It says nothing about costs, about farms smaller or less fertile than the average, or about farmers who sold to traders at lower prices.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of the 2024-25 crop</span>
            <p>In <span id="ex-ha-fy">–</span>, an average hectare gave <b id="ex-ha-yield">–</b> of wheat. At the support price of <b id="ex-ha-msp">–</b> a quintal, that is <b id="ex-ha-gross">–</b>. In <span id="ex-ha-fy0">–</span>, the first year in the table, the same sum was <b id="ex-ha-gross0">–</b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 how to read this: evergreen, human-written -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>How to read these numbers</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p><b>Crop years.</b> Indian agriculture counts from July to June, so that one year holds both seasons: the kharif crop sown with the monsoon (rice, most pulses, cotton, soyabean, groundnut) and the rabi crop sown after it in winter (wheat, gram, mustard). The crop year 2024-25 is July 2024 to June 2025. On this page it is dated at April 2024, as the site dates every financial year.</p>
      <p><b>Estimates, not counts.</b> Nobody weighs India's harvest. Each state measures the area sown from land records and estimates the yield from crop-cutting experiments, where a small plot in a sample of fields is harvested and weighed. The agriculture ministry adds these up and publishes them in stages: first, second and third advance estimates during the year, then a final estimate about a year after the harvest. The advance figures move, sometimes by several million tonnes. This guide keeps the latest advance estimate apart from the final years and draws it as a separate mark.</p>
      <p><b>Units.</b> Grain, oilseeds and sugarcane are in tonnes. Cotton is counted in bales of 170 kg of lint, and jute and mesta in bales of 180 kg. Tea and coffee are in kg. Yield is kg per hectare; cotton yield is kg of lint, and rice yield is kg of milled rice, not of paddy.</p>
      <p><b>Support prices and procurement.</b> The minimum support price (MSP) is the price at which the government promises to buy a crop, announced before sowing on the advice of the Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices. Prices are set for 22 or so crops, but the government buys large amounts of only two, rice and wheat, through the Food Corporation of India and state agencies. That grain is stored in the central pool and issued to ration shops under the National Food Security Act and other schemes. Issues from the pool are called off-take.</p>
      <p><b>Index numbers.</b> The agriculture ministry also publishes index numbers of area, production and yield, weighting each crop by its value. The base has changed three times (the three years ending 1981-82, 1993-94 and 2007-08). Each base is a different ruler; this guide never joins them.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 04 the anatomy: the harvest since 1951. Inputs: farms-production, farms-advance, farms-yield, farms-area -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>The harvest since 1951</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Foodgrains: rice, wheat, coarse cereals and pulses</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-harvest" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>India grows <span id="tt-harvest">–</span> times the grain it grew in 1950-51</h3>
            <div class="sub">Foodgrain production, million tonnes, crop years. The line is the agriculture ministry's final estimates; the open circle is the latest advance estimate, a separate ruler that will be revised.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-harvest">–</div><div class="c" id="pc-harvest">–</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend">
            <button class="chip"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Final estimates</button>
            <button class="chip"><i style="width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:50%;background:#fff;border:1.5px solid #2E4BD8"></i><span id="lg-ae">–</span></button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-harvest" style="position:relative"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Agriculture ministry, as published by the RBI (DBIE report 623; Handbook of Statistics on the Indian Economy).</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Each point is a whole crop year, both seasons. The dips are droughts: 1965-66 and 1966-67, 1979-80, 1987-88, 2002-03 and 2009-10 were all years of a failed monsoon. Population grew too, so grain per person rose far less than the line; this chart is the total.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of the 2025-26 advance estimate</span>
            <p>The final estimate for <span id="ex-hv-fy">–</span> is <b id="ex-hv-fg">–</b>, against <b id="ex-hv-fg0">–</b> in <span id="ex-hv-fy0">–</span>. The ministry's <span id="ex-hv-stage">–</span> for <span id="ex-hv-aey">–</span> put the harvest at <b id="ex-hv-ae">–</b>, a figure that will be revised.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-grains" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Wheat and rice carried the rise; coarse cereals and pulses lagged</h3>
            <div class="sub">Production of each foodgrain, million tonnes, final estimates, with the latest advance estimate as an open circle. Coarse cereals are jowar, bajra, maize, ragi, barley and the small millets.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-grains">–</div><div class="c">rice, latest final year</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-grains" style="position:relative"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Agriculture ministry, as published by the RBI (DBIE report 623).</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The four panels share one scale, so their heights compare directly. Wheat took off after the high-yielding varieties of the mid-1960s, the Green Revolution, first in Punjab, Haryana and western Uttar Pradesh. Pulses, grown mostly on unirrigated land, barely moved for forty years before rising in the last decade.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of the 2024-25 crop</span>
            <p>In <span id="ex-gr-fy">–</span>: rice <b id="ex-gr-rice">–</b>, wheat <b id="ex-gr-wheat">–</b>, coarse cereals <b id="ex-gr-coarse">–</b> and pulses <b id="ex-gr-pulses">–</b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-yield" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Most of the gain came from each hectare giving more</h3>
            <div class="sub">Yield per hectare, kg, final estimates, crop years, 1950-51 on. Rice yield is milled rice.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-yield">–</div><div class="c">wheat yield, times its 1950-51 level</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-yield" style="position:relative"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Agriculture ministry, as published by the RBI (DBIE report 886).</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Yield is the harvest divided by the area under the crop. It rises with better seed, irrigation, fertiliser and timely sowing, and falls with a bad monsoon, pests or heat at the wrong moment. The state charts further down show how far yields still differ inside India: the same land could feed more.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of the 2024-25 crop</span>
            <p>An average hectare gave <b id="ex-yl-wheat">–</b> of wheat, <b id="ex-yl-rice">–</b> of rice and <b id="ex-yl-pulses">–</b> of pulses in <span id="ex-yl-fy">–</span>. In 1950-51 the figures were <b id="ex-yl-wheat0">–</b>, <b id="ex-yl-rice0">–</b> and <b id="ex-yl-pulses0">–</b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-area" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Land under grain has grown little since the 1960s, and coarse cereals lost <span id="tt-area">–</span> of theirs</h3>
            <div class="sub">Area under each foodgrain, million hectares, final estimates, crop years.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-area">–</div><div class="c">million hectares under foodgrains, latest final year</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-area" style="position:relative"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Agriculture ministry, as published by the RBI (DBIE report 625).</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>A hectare that carries two crops in a year is counted twice, once in each crop's area. The fall in coarse cereals is the story of millets: as irrigation spread, farmers moved to rice and wheat, which the government buys at a support price, and to cash crops.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of the 2024-25 crop</span>
            <p>In <span id="ex-ar-fy">–</span>, rice covered <b id="ex-ar-rice">–</b>, wheat <b id="ex-ar-wheat">–</b>, coarse cereals <b id="ex-ar-coarse">–</b> and pulses <b id="ex-ar-pulses">–</b> million hectares. Coarse cereals covered <b id="ex-ar-coarse0">–</b> million hectares in 1950-51.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 05 beyond grain. Inputs: farms-production, farms-area, farms-advance, farms-index-1982/1994/2008 -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">05</span><h2>Beyond grain: oilseeds, sugarcane, fibres and plantations</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Every commercial crop RBI's tables carry</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-crops" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Soyabean grew from almost nothing, cotton leapt after 2002, and jute has stood still for decades</h3>
            <div class="sub">Production of each commercial crop, final estimates, crop years, each panel on its own scale and in its own unit. Open circles: the latest advance estimate.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-crops">–</div><div class="c">million tonnes of sugarcane, latest final year</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-crops" style="position:relative"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Agriculture ministry, Tea Board and Coffee Board, as published by the RBI (DBIE report 624).</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Each panel has its own vertical scale: read the shape, not the height against a neighbour. Oilseeds are nine crops together (groundnut, rapeseed and mustard, soyabean, sunflower, sesame, safflower, niger, castor and linseed). India still imports more than half of the edible oil it eats; the domestic harvest is one side of that.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of the 2024-25 crop</span>
            <p>In <span id="ex-cr-fy">–</span> India grew <b id="ex-cr-sugar">–</b> of sugarcane, <b id="ex-cr-oil">–</b> of oilseeds, <b id="ex-cr-cotton">–</b> of cotton and <b id="ex-cr-tea">–</b> of tea.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-index" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Three rulers for one harvest: the index of all crops on each base</h3>
            <div class="sub">Index of agricultural production, all crops, crop years, on three bases (triennium ending 1981-82, 1993-94 and 2007-08 = 100). Drawn side by side and never joined: each base weights crops by their value in its own years.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-index">–</div><div class="c" id="pc-index">–</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend">
            <button class="chip"><i style="background:#ABB7EF"></i>TE 1981-82 = 100</button>
            <button class="chip"><i style="background:#778AE6"></i>TE 1993-94 = 100</button>
            <button class="chip"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>TE 2007-08 = 100</button>
            <button class="chip"><i style="width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:50%;background:#fff;border:1.5px solid #2E4BD8"></i>Advance estimate</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-index" style="position:relative"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Agriculture ministry, index numbers of agricultural production, as published by the RBI (DBIE reports 459 and 460).</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>An index of 150 means production half as large again as in the base years. On the newest base, fruits and vegetables, plantation crops and spices carry weight alongside grain, so the all-crops index can rise when grain is flat. Compare growth within one line; never read one base's level against another's.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of the 2024-25 crop</span>
            <p>On the 2007-08 base, the all-crops production index stood at <b id="ex-ix-all">–</b> in <span id="ex-ix-fy">–</span>, foodgrains at <b id="ex-ix-fg">–</b> and non-foodgrains at <b id="ex-ix-nfg">–</b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 06 land and inputs. Inputs: farms-landuse -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">06</span><h2>Land, water and fertiliser</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Land use and inputs, All India</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-land" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The farmed area stopped growing long ago; irrigation and double cropping kept rising</h3>
            <div class="sub">Net sown area (land sown at least once in the year), gross sown area (counting each season's crop), and gross irrigated area, million hectares, 1950-51 on.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-land">–</div><div class="c">of gross sown area irrigated, latest year</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-land" style="position:relative"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Agriculture ministry, land use statistics, as published by the RBI (DBIE report 463). Irrigated share computed by India Atlas.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The gap between gross and net sown area is land that carries a second or third crop in the year, usually because it has water in winter. Cropping intensity, gross over net, is the measure of that. Most irrigation is groundwater from tube wells, which is why water tables in Punjab, Haryana and Rajasthan keep falling.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of 2024-25</span>
            <p>India sowed <b id="ex-ld-net">–</b> million hectares at least once and <b id="ex-ld-gross">–</b> counting every season, a cropping intensity of <b id="ex-ld-ci">–</b>. Of the gross area, <b id="ex-ld-irr">–</b> was irrigated, against <b id="ex-ld-irr0">–</b> in 1950-51.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-fert" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Fertiliser per hectare climbed steeply after the Green Revolution, then levelled off</h3>
            <div class="sub">Fertiliser used (nitrogen, phosphate and potash, nutrient tonnes) per hectare of gross sown area, kg, 1950-51 on. Computed by India Atlas from RBI's land-use table.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-fert">–</div><div class="c">kg per hectare, latest year</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-fert" style="position:relative"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Agriculture ministry, as published by the RBI (DBIE report 463). Per hectare computed by India Atlas.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>This is the weight of nutrients, not of the bags: a 45 kg bag of urea holds about 21 kg of nitrogen. Urea is sold at a fixed, heavily subsidised price, so farmers use far more nitrogen than phosphate and potash; agronomists recommend a balance closer to four, two and one. The state chart further down shows how unevenly it is spread.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of 2024-25</span>
            <p>India used <b id="ex-ft-tot">–</b> lakh tonnes of nutrients in <span id="ex-ft-fy">–</span>, about <b id="ex-ft-ha">–</b> per hectare sown, and <b id="ex-ft-pest">–</b> thousand tonnes of pesticides in <span id="ex-ft-pfy">–</span>, the latest year printed.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 07 what the state pays. Inputs: farms-msp, cpialrl-1986 (prices guide, read only) -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">07</span><h2>What the state promises to pay</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Minimum support prices, rupees a quintal</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-msp" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The support price for paddy is <span id="tt-msp">–</span> times what it was in 1975-76</h3>
            <div class="sub">Minimum support price, rupees a quintal (100 kg), fair average quality, by crop year: common paddy (kharif) and wheat (rabi).</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-msp">–</div><div class="c" id="pc-msp">–</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend">
            <button class="chip"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Paddy (common)</button>
            <button class="chip"><i style="background:#C47A16"></i>Wheat</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-msp" style="position:relative"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices and the agriculture ministry, as published by the RBI (DBIE reports 461 and 462).</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Kharif prices are announced around June for the crop then being sown; wheat's in October for the crop sold the next spring. In a few years the price includes a bonus the RBI's notes record (wheat in 1992-93, 1993-94, 1996-97, 2005-06, 2006-07 and 2010-11; paddy in 2006-07 and 2007-08). Paddy is priced as paddy, before milling: about two-thirds of its weight becomes rice.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of the 2026-27 kharif prices</span>
            <p>The paddy price for <span id="ex-ms-py">–</span> is <b id="ex-ms-p">–</b> a quintal, up <b id="ex-ms-pr">–</b> on the year before. Wheat for <span id="ex-ms-wy">–</span> is <b id="ex-ms-w">–</b>, up <b id="ex-ms-wr">–</b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-mspinfl" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Support prices rose faster than farm labourers' cost of living in fewer than half the years</h3>
            <div class="sub">Rise in the paddy and wheat support price on the year before, per cent (bars), and inflation in the consumer price index for agricultural labourers, the average of the twelve monthly year-on-year rates over the crop year July to June (line), 1986-87 = 100 base, crop years <span id="sb-mi-span">–</span>.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-mspinfl">–</div><div class="c">years paddy's rise beat that inflation</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend">
            <button class="chip"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Paddy price rise</button>
            <button class="chip"><i style="background:#C47A16"></i>Wheat price rise</button>
            <button class="chip"><i style="background:#161A22;height:2px"></i>Farm labourers' inflation</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-mspinfl" style="position:relative"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Support prices as published by the RBI (DBIE 461); CPI for agricultural labourers, Labour Bureau (the prices guide's bundle cpialrl-1986). Rises and averages computed by India Atlas.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Where a bar stands above the line, the support price rose faster than the cost of living of a farm labourer's household that year. That says nothing about the cost of growing the crop: seed, fertiliser, diesel and hired labour. The Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices estimates those costs state by state, but the RBI's tables do not carry them, so this guide cannot show the margin. We do not know from these tables whether farming a quintal became more or less profitable.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of the 2023-24 crop</span>
            <p>Of the <b id="ex-mi-n">–</b> crop years compared, the paddy price rose faster than farm labourers' inflation in <b id="ex-mi-p">–</b> and wheat in <b id="ex-mi-w">–</b>. In <span id="ex-mi-fy">–</span> paddy rose <b id="ex-mi-pl">–</b>, wheat <b id="ex-mi-wl">–</b>, and the labourers' index <b id="ex-mi-cl">–</b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-mspall" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Over ten years, oilseeds and cotton were given the biggest increases</h3>
            <div class="sub">Rise in each crop's minimum support price over its latest ten crop years, per cent, with the latest price in rupees a quintal. Crops no longer priced (soyabean black, last priced in 2016-17) are left out.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-mspall">–</div><div class="c" id="pc-mspall">–</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-mspall" style="position:relative"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Support prices as published by the RBI (DBIE reports 461 and 462). Ten-year rise computed by India Atlas.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Raising the price of oilseeds faster than that of rice and wheat is one way the government has tried to pull farmers towards crops India imports. Pulses, another import, were given rises close to those for paddy and wheat. A price only works where someone buys at it: the government buys pulses and oilseeds in much smaller amounts than rice and wheat.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of the 2026-27 prices</span>
            <p>The largest ten-year rise was for <span id="ex-ma-top">–</span>, the smallest for <span id="ex-ma-bot">–</span>; paddy rose <b id="ex-ma-p">–</b> and wheat <b id="ex-ma-w">–</b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

