        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-statefert" style="position:relative"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Agriculture ministry, Agricultural Statistics at a Glance, as published in the RBI's Handbook of Statistics on Indian States (table 94; DBIE SDMX dataset AGG_STAT_IS_RN, via the RBI Innovation Hub service).</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Per hectare figures follow irrigation and crop mix: irrigated rice and wheat take far more fertiliser than rain-fed millets or pulses, and the hill and north-eastern states, where much farming is organic by default, use very little. A high figure is not the same as good practice; overuse of nitrogen wastes money and leaches into groundwater.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of 2020-21</span>
            <p>In <span id="ex-sf-fy">–</span>, <span id="ex-sf-top">–</span> used the most, <b id="ex-sf-topv">–</b> per hectare, against an All India average of <b id="ex-sf-ai">–</b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 10 the monsoon. Inputs: farms-production (foodgrains), climate-cru-monthly (climate guide, read only) -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">10</span><h2>The monsoon and the harvest</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">June to September rainfall and the year's foodgrain output</span></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>About <span id="mn-unirr">–</span> of India's gross sown area has no irrigation, and even irrigated land depends on the rain that refills wells and reservoirs. The four monsoon months, June to September, bring most of the year's rain. A weak monsoon cuts the kharif crop at once and the rabi crop through dry soil and empty tanks. For the full picture of rain and heat, see the <a href="/guides/climate/">climate guide</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-monsoon" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The worst harvests followed dry monsoons, but not every dry monsoon cut the harvest</h3>
            <div class="sub">Each dot is a crop year since 1951-52: across, June to September rainfall over India, per cent above or below its 1951-2000 average; up, the change in foodgrain production on the year before, per cent, final estimates. Rainfall: CRU gridded observations, as in the climate guide; not the India Meteorological Department's own monsoon figure.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-monsoon">–</div><div class="c">of the ten driest monsoons were followed by a smaller harvest</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend">
            <button class="chip"><i style="width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:50%;background:#B9C3F3"></i>1951-52 to 1999-2000</button>
            <button class="chip"><i style="width:9px;height:9px;border-radius:50%;background:#2E4BD8"></i>2000-01 on</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-monsoon" style="position:relative"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Foodgrain production, agriculture ministry via the RBI (DBIE 623); rainfall, CRU TS 4.10, University of East Anglia, via the World Bank Climate Change Knowledge Portal (the climate guide's bundle climate-cru-monthly). Departures and changes computed by India Atlas.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://climateknowledgeportal.worldbank.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Dots to the left had a dry monsoon; dots low down a smaller harvest than the year before. The crop year 2002-03 lines up with the monsoon of 2002, its kharif season. The link is loose: a dry June can be made up in August, irrigation has grown, and a good harvest the year before makes the next one's change look worse. A gridded estimate of rain over the whole country is not the farmer's rain gauge.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of the 2024 monsoon</span>
            <p>The driest monsoon in the record was <span id="ex-mn-dy">–</span>, <b id="ex-mn-dv">–</b> below average; foodgrain output that crop year changed by <b id="ex-mn-dc">–</b>. In <span id="ex-mn-ly">–</span>, rainfall was <b id="ex-mn-lv">–</b> the average and output changed by <b id="ex-mn-lc">–</b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 11 misreadings -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">11</span><h2>How to misread these numbers</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li><span class="no">1</span><div><h3>"A record harvest this year."</h3><p>The first figures for a year are advance estimates, made while the crop is still in the field or just cut. They are revised over the next year, up or down. A record on the second advance estimate is a forecast. This guide shows the final years as a line and the latest advance estimate apart.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">2</span><div><h3>"Farmers get the MSP."</h3><p>The support price is what the government pays when it buys. It buys mostly rice and wheat, mostly in a few states. A farmer elsewhere, or growing another crop, sells at whatever traders pay, which can be well below it.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">3</span><div><h3>"The MSP went up, so farmers earn more."</h3><p>A higher price per quintal can be eaten by higher costs of diesel, fertiliser and labour. The RBI's tables carry the prices but not the costs, so this guide cannot show the margin. We do not know from these numbers whether a quintal became more profitable.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">4</span><div><h3>"Overflowing granaries mean nobody goes hungry."</h3><p>Stocks are grain the government holds, not grain people eat. Hunger depends on whether a family can get and afford food: ration cards, prices, wages. Large stocks can sit beside poor diets, and they cost the government money to keep.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">5</span><div><h3>"Rice yield is 2,900 kg, so a hectare gives 2.9 tonnes of paddy."</h3><p>Rice yield is milled rice. Paddy, the grain as harvested, weighs about half as much again. Comparing rice yield with the paddy price, or with another country's paddy yield, overstates or understates by a third.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">6</span><div><h3>"Madhya Pradesh's wheat jumped in 2000."</h3><p>Some state lines break because the state changed. Madhya Pradesh, Bihar and Uttar Pradesh lost territory in 2000, Andhra Pradesh in 2014. Compare a state only within one shape of its map.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">7</span><div><h3>"Cotton output is 30 million tonnes."</h3><p>Cotton is counted in bales of 170 kg, so 300 lakh bales is about 5 million tonnes of lint. Jute bales are 180 kg. Read the unit before comparing crops.</p></div></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

