  <footer class="provenance">
    <h2 class="prov-h">Sources, method and revisions</h2>
    <div class="cols">
      <div>
        <h6>Sources of record</h6>
        <p>Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare (crop area, production and yield, index numbers, land use, inputs, state-wise production, minimum support prices), the Tea Board and the Coffee Board, and the Ministry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution (procurement, off-take and stocks), as the Reserve Bank of India publishes them in its Database on Indian Economy and its Handbooks. Read from the Atlas's local, read-only mirror of DBIE; report 190 (commercial-crop area), the Handbook's printed tables and two SDMX datasets from the Reserve Bank Innovation Hub's DBIE service. Data vintage <b id="prov-vintage">–</b>.</p>
        <p>Every cell the Data Bank holds was checked against the RBI's own printed table at its printed precision: 16,800 cells across twelve tables, all equal. Checked by hand: rice 2024-25, 150,184 thousand tonnes (the Handbook prints 1,502 lakh tonnes); cotton 2023-24, 325.22 lakh bales, the ministry's final figure; paddy MSP 2024-25 ₹2,300 and wheat 2025-26 ₹2,585, the government's announced prices; wheat stocks on 1 April 2025, 11.8 million tonnes, as the food ministry reported.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h6>Method</h6>
        <p>Values are stored once each (DBIE repeats every value once per display unit), in the units the RBI prints: thousand tonnes, thousand bales, thousand hectares, kg per hectare, lakh tonnes, rupees a quintal. Crop years are dated at April of their first year. Shares, per hectare figures, the hectare's value at the support price, rainfall departures and the crop-year average of farm labourers' inflation are computed by India Atlas and labelled as such.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h6>Revisions and breaks</h6>
        <p>Never joined: the latest advance estimate and the final years; index numbers on the three bases; states before and after a split (Madhya Pradesh, Bihar and Uttar Pradesh in 2000, Andhra Pradesh in 2014, Jammu and Kashmir in 2019). Left out: the RBI's stock figures for 1950-51 to 1970-71, printed at more than India's whole harvest, whose unit cannot be read; the SDMX totals that add tonnes to bales or prices of different crops. The part year after the last full year (stocks on 31 May 2026) is shown apart.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h6>This guide</h6>
        <p>Version 1.0, built October 2026. Revised when the RBI updates its agriculture tables, usually with the Handbook each September, and when the ministry's estimates move. Agricultural credit is in the <a href="/guides/credit/">credit guide</a>; rural wages in the <a href="/guides/cpi/">prices guide</a>; each state's farm figures in the <a href="/guides/states/">states guide</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
